WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.581
The interview deck makes
one honest limit explicit:

2
00:00:03.581 --> 00:00:08.590
published Federal HR 2.0 scope centers
on the federal employee lifecycle,

3
00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:11.875
so a contractor authority
decision is required.

4
00:00:11.875 --> 00:00:16.437
In the processes I have observed,
contractors are tracked in COR spreadsheets.

5
00:00:16.437 --> 00:00:18.876
This companion goes one step further.

6
00:00:18.876 --> 00:00:23.392
It asks which record should be authoritative
for a contractor, and it adds an

7
00:00:23.392 --> 00:00:28.125
option the main deck doesn't name: the
agency's contractor suitability register.

8
00:00:28.125 --> 00:00:31.900
The one-sentence version: don't
wait for one contractor feed.

9
00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:37.075
Most agencies maintain contractor
information in several operational contexts.

10
00:00:37.075 --> 00:00:41.418
Evaluate them as candidate authorities,
give each fact one authority,

11
00:00:41.418 --> 00:00:45.088
and hang the record on an
accountable federal sponsor.

12
00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:47.972
Three facts that don't fit together.

13
00:00:47.972 --> 00:00:49.184
First, scope.

14
00:00:49.184 --> 00:00:55.263
The Federal HR 2.0 memo of December 10,
2025 scopes Core HCM to personnel action

15
00:00:55.263 --> 00:01:01.435
processing, the employee system of record
and the federal employee lifecycle.

16
00:01:01.435 --> 00:01:05.735
The June 2026 Oracle award
covers civilian employees.

17
00:01:05.735 --> 00:01:09.760
Neither establishes contractor
treatment in the sources reviewed.

18
00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.749
Oracle Fusion HCM can hold
contingent workers as a product,

19
00:01:13.749 --> 00:01:16.110
but the program doesn't commit to it.

20
00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.123
Second, what I have observed.

21
00:01:18.123 --> 00:01:22.560
In the processes I have observed,
contractors are tracked in COR spreadsheets,

22
00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.048
and many of their accounts
come from tickets.

23
00:01:25.048 --> 00:01:28.100
Based on current visibility,
nothing authoritative

24
00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.360
tells identity that a contractor's
engagement has ended.

25
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:32.848
Third, guidance.

26
00:01:32.848 --> 00:01:36.758
Federal lifecycle guidance,
including GSA's ILM Playbook,

27
00:01:36.758 --> 00:01:42.543
calls for an authoritative personnel source
for identity creation and termination,

28
00:01:42.543 --> 00:01:46.210
and CDM's master user record
must include contractors.

29
00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.104
Employee HR may not carry them,

30
00:01:48.104 --> 00:01:52.423
so contractors need an equivalent
authoritative personnel source.

31
00:01:52.423 --> 00:01:54.870
Something else has to be the authority,

32
00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:57.847
and it needs the same
rigor as the HR record.

33
00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:02.400
The useful finding: most agencies
aren't starting from zero.

34
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.937
Contractor information usually appears
in several operational contexts.

35
00:02:06.937 --> 00:02:12.438
COR spreadsheets: real, but kept per
COR, with no common format or timing.

36
00:02:12.438 --> 00:02:14.376
They're evidence, not a source.

37
00:02:14.376 --> 00:02:18.408
The contractor suitability
register: many agencies keep one,

38
00:02:18.408 --> 00:02:21.175
under personnel security or contracting.

39
00:02:21.175 --> 00:02:24.855
It typically holds contractors
working on agency contracts,

40
00:02:24.855 --> 00:02:27.813
with the suitability
determination and its date.

41
00:02:27.813 --> 00:02:31.664
Its coverage, contract
identifiers, lifecycle fields,

42
00:02:31.664 --> 00:02:35.687
owner, interface and update
cadence require validation.

43
00:02:35.687 --> 00:02:39.859
PIV enrollment: the credentialing
system enrolls employees,

44
00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:42.150
contractors and other personnel.

45
00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:46.107
It covers identity proofing and
background-investigation workflows,

46
00:02:46.107 --> 00:02:49.125
and the card management
system issues the credential.

47
00:02:49.125 --> 00:02:53.047
That makes it a candidate source
for proofing and credential status;

48
00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:57.313
its authoritative fields and population
coverage require confirmation.

49
00:02:57.313 --> 00:03:02.119
What's usually missing is any link from
these to Entra ID or Active Directory:

50
00:03:02.119 --> 00:03:04.637
contractor accounts come from tickets.

51
00:03:04.637 --> 00:03:09.159
Core HCM's Contingent Worker type
is a possible fourth source later,

52
00:03:09.159 --> 00:03:11.662
if the agency chooses to configure it.

53
00:03:11.662 --> 00:03:16.712
It won't exist before the agency's
Core HCM onboarding wave.

54
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:21.068
Same four sources, against
the facts identity needs.

55
00:03:21.068 --> 00:03:24.767
COR spreadsheets exist now, but
they're not a governed record.

56
00:03:24.767 --> 00:03:27.592
Use them to reconcile, not as a source.

57
00:03:27.592 --> 00:03:31.072
Core HCM's Contingent Worker
type would give a person

58
00:03:31.072 --> 00:03:34.630
record and an assignment with
dates, like an employee's.

59
00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:39.644
But it has no contract or vetting
data, it sits outside the Federal HR

60
00:03:39.644 --> 00:03:44.568
2.0 program scope, and current
planning puts it no earlier than 2027.

61
00:03:44.568 --> 00:03:49.072
Where an agency keeps a contractor
suitability register, it is the only

62
00:03:49.072 --> 00:03:53.729
one that links a person to an agency
contract with a suitability decision.

63
00:03:53.729 --> 00:03:57.064
What varies by agency: who
owns it, how it's accessed,

64
00:03:57.064 --> 00:04:01.618
how fast it's updated, and whether
it records the end of an engagement.

65
00:04:01.618 --> 00:04:04.566
PIV enrollment covers
everyone with a badge and

66
00:04:04.566 --> 00:04:08.030
carries proofing and
background-investigation status.

67
00:04:08.030 --> 00:04:12.877
But it manages a card, not an identity
with an organization and entitlements,

68
00:04:12.877 --> 00:04:15.955
and it misses anyone who
works without a PIV card.

69
00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:17.730
So the answer isn't to pick one.

70
00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:20.030
It's to give each fact one authority.

71
00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:21.380
Next slide.

72
00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:25.568
I propose this working position;
it is not yet a decision.

73
00:04:25.568 --> 00:04:30.831
It's the same discipline as the employee
design: a named authority for every fact.

74
00:04:30.831 --> 00:04:35.519
I propose the contractor suitability
register as the candidate authority for

75
00:04:35.519 --> 00:04:38.218
existence, the contract
link and suitability,

76
00:04:38.218 --> 00:04:40.705
subject to the questions
on the next slide.

77
00:04:40.705 --> 00:04:43.357
If it also records the
end of the engagement,

78
00:04:43.357 --> 00:04:48.293
it becomes the contractor's leaver signal,
the equivalent of a separation action.

79
00:04:48.293 --> 00:04:50.093
That's the first thing to confirm.

80
00:04:50.093 --> 00:04:54.692
PIV enrollment is the authority
for proofing and credential status.

81
00:04:54.692 --> 00:04:57.130
Identity reads it and cross-checks it.

82
00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.415
A badge with no contractor record,

83
00:04:59.415 --> 00:05:02.680
or a contractor record with
no badge, is a finding.

84
00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:06.251
The accountable party is a
federal employee sponsor,

85
00:05:06.251 --> 00:05:08.443
normally the COR, mastered in HR.

86
00:05:08.443 --> 00:05:13.615
The contractor's organization placement is
an explicit authoritative assignment; the

87
00:05:13.615 --> 00:05:18.504
sponsor's organization is a default only
where policy confirms that relationship.

88
00:05:18.504 --> 00:05:20.861
I propose the enterprise person key,

89
00:05:20.861 --> 00:05:24.317
the same as for employees,
proposed for confirmation.

90
00:05:24.317 --> 00:05:29.171
Many agencies already issue it to
contractors along with their PIV card,

91
00:05:29.171 --> 00:05:31.193
for example as a Mainframe PIN.

92
00:05:31.193 --> 00:05:35.013
Where they do, a contractor is
keyed exactly like an employee,

93
00:05:35.013 --> 00:05:39.522
and keeps the same key on a new contract;
in the proposed canonical model,

94
00:05:39.522 --> 00:05:44.642
conversion to federal employment creates
a new affiliation for the same person.

95
00:05:44.642 --> 00:05:48.656
On the right: access ends at
the earliest of five events.

96
00:05:48.656 --> 00:05:52.444
The last two close the gap the
Digital Worker playbook leaves open:

97
00:05:52.444 --> 00:05:54.580
what happens when the sponsor leaves.

98
00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:59.005
The sponsor is a live reference
into HR, not a name on a form.

99
00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:04.162
Six things to establish before calling
a suitability register an authority.

100
00:06:04.162 --> 00:06:07.499
The first two decide whether it
can be the leaver signal at all.

101
00:06:07.499 --> 00:06:10.788
Owner: is it contracting
or personnel security?

102
00:06:10.788 --> 00:06:16.037
The owner has to accept identity as a
named consumer, just as I'm asking HR to.

103
00:06:16.037 --> 00:06:21.089
Content: if the register records only
the suitability decision and its date,

104
00:06:21.089 --> 00:06:23.976
it's a vetting source,
not a lifecycle source.

105
00:06:23.976 --> 00:06:27.937
Then the end date has to come from the
contract record or from the sponsor.

106
00:06:27.937 --> 00:06:32.126
Freshness: if the register is keyed
by hand from COR spreadsheets,

107
00:06:32.126 --> 00:06:37.075
it inherits their lag, and every separation
delay carries over to contractors.

108
00:06:37.075 --> 00:06:39.645
Privacy: which SORN covers the register,

109
00:06:39.645 --> 00:06:43.538
and does a routine use cover
disclosure to identity systems?

110
00:06:43.538 --> 00:06:46.913
It's the same question the
main deck asks for HR data.

111
00:06:46.913 --> 00:06:51.002
Join on the key: if contractors
may already get the enterprise

112
00:06:51.002 --> 00:06:54.450
key with their PIV card,
confirm coverage and timing.

113
00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:57.388
The question is also whether
the register records it.

114
00:06:57.388 --> 00:07:02.888
If it does, the register, PIV enrollment
and the directories join on one key.

115
00:07:02.888 --> 00:07:06.788
If not, the first job is a crosswalk
from the register to the key.

116
00:07:06.788 --> 00:07:11.225
Coverage: who works without a PIV
card and isn't in the register?

117
00:07:11.225 --> 00:07:13.975
Those people still need
a sponsor-only path.

118
00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:17.915
Start with what already
exists, and start read-only.

119
00:07:17.915 --> 00:07:21.424
Now: reconcile the register
and PIV enrollment

120
00:07:21.424 --> 00:07:25.202
against the contractor
accounts in AD and Entra ID.

121
00:07:25.202 --> 00:07:30.121
The output is unmatched accounts,
unmatched contractor records, credential

122
00:07:30.121 --> 00:07:35.115
mismatches, and any records the available
source shows as expired or inactive.

123
00:07:35.115 --> 00:07:37.278
Nothing is written to any directory.

124
00:07:37.278 --> 00:07:41.265
That measures the problem before
anyone is asked to change a process.

125
00:07:41.265 --> 00:07:45.927
Before Core HCM: I propose that the
register plus sponsor attestation

126
00:07:45.927 --> 00:07:51.152
become the contractor source, once the
contractor authority decision is made.

127
00:07:51.152 --> 00:07:56.091
Ship the leaver trigger first, because
that's where the risk is, then the joiner.

128
00:07:56.091 --> 00:07:59.715
Mismatches between badges
and records become findings.

129
00:07:59.715 --> 00:08:05.103
At Core HCM go-live: decide whether to
configure the Contingent Worker type.

130
00:08:05.103 --> 00:08:08.707
If the agency adopts it, it holds
the person and the assignment,

131
00:08:08.707 --> 00:08:12.877
and the register stays the authority
for the contract and suitability.

132
00:08:12.877 --> 00:08:16.053
Either way, the identity
pipeline doesn't change.

133
00:08:16.053 --> 00:08:19.102
It reads one canonical
record whatever the source.

134
00:08:19.102 --> 00:08:22.530
The ask is small: make the
contractor authority decision,

135
00:08:22.530 --> 00:08:27.561
evaluating the suitability register,
sponsorship, and contingent-worker records;

136
00:08:27.561 --> 00:08:31.864
name its owner; validate the planning
assumptions with the data owners;

137
00:08:31.864 --> 00:08:35.802
and give read access to the register
for a shadow reconciliation.
